<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      allowBackup is off, and that is a decision rather than a default.

      Android's auto-backup copies an application's data directory to the
      account holder's Drive. For FamilyOS that directory is the WebView's
      IndexedDB: a household's records, and the wrapped key material beside
      them. The wrapping is sound, and it is only as strong as what wraps it —
      `auth/lock.js` allows a PIN of four digits, so ten thousand candidates
      stand between an exfiltrated store and the records in it. Rate limiting
      guards the keypad, not a copy of the file. An application whose premise
      is "encrypted, on this device" should not put that copy on somebody
      else's server by default.

      READ THIS BEFORE SHIPPING. What it costs is not small, and an earlier
      version of this comment got it wrong by saying the recovery phrase and
      Drive sync cover it. They do not, here. The recovery phrase restores a
      *key*, not data — Settings says so in as many words — and Drive sync
      needs Google sign-in, which does not work inside a WebView (see
      docs/CAPACITOR_SETUP.md). So on a native build today, with this off,
      a lost or wiped phone means the records are gone.

      That is a real trade-off between a weak-PIN exposure and losing
      everything, and it belongs to the household rather than to this file.
      It is off because the exposure is silent and the loss is not: nobody is
      told their key material went to Drive, and everybody notices a lost
      phone. Turn it back on by changing `false` to `true` below; nothing else
      depends on it.

      usesCleartextTraffic is stated rather than inherited. It is already the
      platform default above API 27, and writing it down means a later
      targetSdk change cannot silently flip it.
    -->
    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme">

        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation|density"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:exported="true">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths"></meta-data>
        </provider>
    </application>

    <!-- Permissions -->

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
        Location, for Phase 15: safe zones and the "where is everyone" screen.
        Both are asked for at runtime by @capacitor/geolocation, and the app
        works without them — every screen degrades to "no reading on this
        device" rather than breaking.

        COARSE is listed as well as FINE deliberately. Android lets a person
        grant the approximate one only, and a household that would rather say
        "the right neighbourhood" than "the right doorstep" should be able to:
        a coarse fix still decides a 3 km zone, and `domain/geo.js` refuses to
        place it against a 200 m one instead of guessing.

        ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION is NOT here, and its absence is the design.
        It would need a foreground service, a persistent notification and a
        Play policy declaration, and it would change what this application is:
        something that watches a family rather than something a family opens.
        docs/LOCATION.md says so, and nothing in the app claims otherwise.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" />

    <!--
        Declared not-required so the Play listing does not exclude devices
        without GPS hardware. The feature is optional here in the real sense:
        a device that cannot fix a position gets a screen that says so.
    -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.location.gps" android:required="false" />
</manifest>
